            "text": "Most hemp protein powders are about 50 percent protein, so a typical 30 g serving gives 13 to 15 g of protein. Concentrated versions such as Manitoba Harvest Max Protein reach about 20 g per serving. High-fiber versions give less, around 9 to 11 g. Always check the serving size, because labels range from 10 g tablespoons to 36 g scoops."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is hemp protein better than pea protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For fiber, minerals and minimal processing, yes. For protein per scoop, protein per calorie and usually cost per gram of protein, pea isolate wins. Their amino acid profiles complement each other, with hemp low in lysine and pea low in methionine, so mixing roughly one part hemp to two parts pea gives you the strengths of both in one shake."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will hemp protein make me fail a drug test?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Hemp seed foods come from industrial hemp varieties bred for very low THC, and the seed itself carries very little, so normal servings are not expected to cause a problem for most people. If you are tested under a zero-tolerance policy, such as in tested sport or certain jobs, ask your testing body before using any hemp product, since their rules are what count."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does hemp protein have a lot of fiber?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
