        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Plant Protein",
    tags: [
      "best hemp protein powder",
      "hemp protein powder",
      "hemp protein",
      "hemp seed protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Buyer's Guide" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Hemp Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Six hemp protein powders sold on Amazon, ranked on what a scoop really delivers, with an honest answer on why most of them give you 13 to 15 g of protein and a lot of fiber.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#what-it-is">What Hemp Protein Is</a>
      <a href="#density">The Density Problem</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#fiber">Fiber: Hemp's Real Strength</a>
      <a href="#hemp-vs-pea">Hemp vs Pea</a>
      <a href="#protein-quality">Protein Quality</a>
      <a href="#who-its-for">Who It Is For</a>
      <a href="#organic-metals">Organic and Heavy Metals</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#how-to-use">How to Use It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Hemp protein is a whole food pretending to be a protein powder. Most of it is simply hemp seed with the oil pressed out and the cake milled fine, which is why a typical 30 g scoop gives you only 13 to 15 g of protein, plus 6 to 8 g of fiber, magnesium, iron and some omega fats. That makes it an excellent smoothie booster and a poor stand-alone muscle shake. Buy it for what it is, and it is one of the least processed proteins on the shelf. Buy it expecting a pea isolate, and you will need nearly twice the powder.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Hemp Protein Powder Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hemp protein starts as hemp seed from industrial hemp varieties of Cannabis sativa. The seed is cold-pressed to remove most of its oil, and the pressed cake that is left is milled and sifted into a fine green-brown powder. Nothing is chemically extracted in the standard product; several brands say they use no hexane. The result is roughly 50 percent protein by weight, with the rest made up of fiber, residual fat and minerals.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is very different from how pea, soy or whey isolates are made. Those go through extra steps that strip out almost everything except protein, ending up at 80 to 90 percent protein. Hemp powders mostly skip that step. A few, such as Manitoba Harvest's Max Protein, use a more concentrated hemp protein, which the listing says is about 70 percent protein by weight, but even that is less concentrated than a typical pea isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The main protein in hemp seed is edestin, a storage protein that digests reasonably well, alongside albumin. Hemp seed protein is also naturally free of the major allergens most people worry about: no dairy, soy, gluten in the seed itself, or egg. Check each bag for cross-contact statements, since some are packed in facilities that handle tree nuts or other allergens.
          </p>

          <p>
            And no, it will not get you high. Hemp seed foods come from varieties bred for very low THC, and the seed itself carries very little; trace amounts come from contact with the plant during harvest. If you are subject to strict drug testing, such as in tested sport or certain jobs, check with your testing body, because zero-tolerance rules are about what a test detects, not what is legal to buy. For most buyers, this is not a practical concern.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="density" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Density Problem: Why 15 g per Scoop Is Normal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most hemp protein labels list a serving of about 30 g, usually around 4 tablespoons, delivering 13 to 15 g of protein. Navitas lists 13 g of protein in 30 g; Nutiva, Anthony's and 365 by Whole Foods Market list about 15 g in 30 g. Terrasoul's panel lists 5 g in a 10 g tablespoon, which works out to about 15 g in 30 g. Only the concentrated Manitoba Harvest Max Protein clears 20 g, in a serving of about 30 to 32 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with pea protein isolate, where a scoop of roughly 30 to 33 g typically gives 24 to 27 g of protein. To get 25 g of protein from a standard hemp powder you need about 50 to 58 g of powder, close to two full servings. That also means close to 200 calories or more, 10 to 14 g of fiber and a noticeably thicker drink. For a smoothie that is fine. For a quick shaker bottle after the gym, it is a lot of sludge.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per 30 g (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder for 25 g protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories at 25 g protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Manitoba Harvest Max Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g per 30-32 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 175</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutiva, Anthony's, 365</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 50 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 185-200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Navitas Organics</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 13 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 58 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 230</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical pea isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24-27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110-125</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is not a flaw so much as a description of the product. Hemp powder is a minimally processed seed food, and everything that is not protein is fiber, fat and minerals rather than filler. The mistake is comparing a hemp scoop with a pea or whey scoop as if they were the same kind of product. They are not, and the honest way to compare them is by grams of protein, not by scoops.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your main goal is hitting a daily protein target, start from that number. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> helps you set it. Then decide whether hemp fills part of that target inside meals, which it does well, or whether you need a denser powder for the shake itself, which is usually pea, soy or a blend.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Hemp Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product below is a hemp protein powder sold on Amazon US at the time of writing, each from a different brand. We ranked them on protein per serving, fiber, organic certification, ingredient list and approximate cost per 25 g of protein. Label figures are per the label or retailer listing at the time of writing and can vary between batches and bag sizes. We have not tasted or lab-tested these powders; texture and taste notes are buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="fiber" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fiber: Hemp's Real Selling Point
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where hemp genuinely beats almost every other protein powder is fiber. Standard hemp protein lists roughly 6 to 8 g of fiber per 30 g serving, mostly insoluble. Manitoba Harvest's Balanced Protein + Fiber lists 8 g in a 36 g serving on one retailer panel, and Nutiva's Hi-Fiber version lists about 11 to 12 g of fiber per serving alongside 11 g of protein. By contrast, whey has none and pea isolate has very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because most adults eat well short of the commonly recommended 25 to 38 g of fiber a day. A hemp smoothie can close a meaningful share of that gap while adding protein, which is a combination you rarely get from one ingredient. Fiber also adds bulk and slows digestion, which many people find helps a smoothie feel more like a meal than a drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downside is the same fact viewed from the other side. If you add 10 to 14 g of fiber in one go to a diet that is used to much less, you may get bloating or gas for the first week or two. Start with half a serving, drink enough water, and build up. People with irritable bowel syndrome or other digestive conditions should introduce it carefully and talk to a doctor or dietitian if symptoms persist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hemp also brings minerals. Navitas lists about 249 mg of magnesium, around 60 percent of the daily value, in a 30 g serving, and Manitoba Harvest's Balanced Protein + Fiber panel lists 296 mg of magnesium and 8 mg of iron in 36 g. Those are real nutritional contributions. They are also a reason not to stack hemp with a high-dose mineral supplement without checking totals.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy hemp for protein plus fiber, not protein alone.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you want the most protein per scoop, buy pea or soy isolate. If you want a single, minimally processed ingredient that adds protein, fiber, magnesium and iron to a smoothie, hemp is one of the best things you can buy.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hemp-vs-pea" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Hemp vs Pea Protein: Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pea protein isolate is the default plant protein for muscle building, and for good reason. It is far denser, typically around 80 percent protein, has a solid amino acid profile with plenty of lysine, and costs little per gram. Our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein powder guide</a> ranks the best options, several of which give 24 to 27 g of protein in about 120 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hemp wins on fiber, minerals, omega fats and minimal processing. It also has a milder, nuttier taste than many unflavored pea isolates, although buyers describe it as earthy or grassy, and its texture is grittier. Pea wins on protein per scoop, protein per calorie, leucine per serving and usually cost per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hemp protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pea protein isolate</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
