                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The most useful point is that the two complement each other. Hemp is lowest in lysine; pea is rich in lysine and lowest in methionine, which hemp supplies reasonably well. Mixing them, for example one part hemp to two parts pea, gives you a more complete amino acid profile, more fiber than pea alone and more protein per scoop than hemp alone. Many commercial blends in our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein guide</a> combine pea with other plant sources for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            So, which should you buy? If you only buy one plant protein and your goal is muscle, buy pea or soy; our <a href="/best-soy-protein-powder/">soy protein powder guide</a> covers the other high-quality option. If you mainly want to boost the nutrition of smoothies and oats, buy hemp. If you want both, buy one bag of each and mix them; it costs little and covers the weaknesses of both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Protein Quality: Lysine, PDCAAS and Leucine
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most cited study on hemp protein quality is House, Neufeld and Leson in the Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry in 2010. They measured digestibility and the protein digestibility-corrected amino acid score, or PDCAAS, for commercial hemp products from Western Canada. Hemp seed meal, the closest match to standard hemp protein powder, scored roughly 46 to 51 percent; dehulled hemp seed scored 63 to 66 percent. Lysine was the first limiting amino acid in every product.
          </p>

          <p>
            For context, whey and soy isolates score at or near the top of the PDCAAS scale, and pea protein typically scores well above hemp meal. The authors concluded that hemp protein is equal to or better than certain grains, nuts and some pulses, which is a fair description: hemp is a respectable plant protein, not a top-tier one. Removing the hull improved digestibility, which is part of why concentrated hemp proteins perform better than whole-seed meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine tells a similar story. Published amino acid data suggest hemp protein carries somewhat less leucine per gram of protein than pea and clearly less than whey. Combined with the low protein per scoop, a single 30 g serving of standard hemp powder delivers only about 1 g of leucine by our rough estimate, well below the 2 to 3 g per meal often linked to a strong muscle-building response.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes hemp useless for active people. It means hemp works best as part of a protein-rich meal or blended with a denser protein, rather than as your only post-workout shake. In a mixed diet with legumes, grains, soy or animal foods, hemp's lysine gap is easily covered. On a strict vegan diet, pairing hemp with pea, soy or lentils over the day is the simple fix, and our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> guide explains the trade-offs in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-its-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Who Hemp Protein Is For, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you want a single-ingredient, minimally processed protein</strong> to add to smoothies, oats, yogurt or baking. Hemp is about as close to a whole food as protein powder gets, and the organic options here are just milled hemp seed. It suits people who avoid isolates on principle or want fewer ultra-processed ingredients.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you struggle to eat enough fiber</strong>. A serving of hemp can deliver a quarter of a typical daily fiber target alongside protein, magnesium and iron. <strong>Buy it if you need a dairy-free, soy-free, gluten-free powder</strong> and pea does not agree with you; check the facility statement on the bag if allergies are serious.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Skip it if you want a quick, light post-workout shake</strong> or you are cutting calories hard. You will need roughly 50 g of powder and around 200 calories to reach 25 g of protein. A pea isolate, a soy isolate or, if you eat dairy, a whey isolate gets you there in half the calories. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">weight-loss protein guide</a> ranks powders on protein per calorie.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Be cautious if you are on a low-fiber diet</strong> for medical reasons, have a sensitive gut, or take medication that interacts with minerals or fiber. Hemp seed also contains omega fats, and while the amounts are modest, people on blood thinners or with a medical condition should ask their doctor or a registered dietitian before adding any supplement. Nothing here is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="organic-metals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Organic, Heavy Metals and Sourcing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most hemp protein on Amazon is USDA Organic, and all six of our picks are sold as organic. Manitoba Harvest's Max Protein, Nutiva, Navitas, Anthony's, 365 by Whole Foods Market and Terrasoul all describe their hemp as organic in their listings; check for the USDA seal on the specific bag. Organic certification controls synthetic pesticides and fertilizers in farming. It does not test for heavy metals, which come mainly from soil.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are worth taking seriously with hemp specifically. The hemp plant is good at drawing metals out of soil, so good that it has been studied for cleaning up contaminated land. That does not mean food-grade hemp seed from clean farmland is high in metals, but it does mean sourcing and testing matter. In October 2025 Consumer Reports tested 23 protein products for lead and, per news coverage, found plant-based products averaged far more lead than dairy-based ones. CR's list did not include the hemp powders ranked here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sourcing varies. Manitoba Harvest is a Canadian hemp-food brand from Winnipeg, and Canadian hemp seed is common across the category. Terrasoul's own pages give conflicting origin details, Canada in one place and Lithuania on its foodservice page, so check the bag. If a brand will share recent heavy-metal test results by lot, that is worth more than an organic seal for this particular question.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practical steps: rotate protein sources rather than relying on one plant powder every day for years, prefer brands that publish or share certificates of analysis, and keep hemp to one or two servings a day. Our explainer on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers lead, cadmium and arsenic across the category, and our <a href="/best-organic-protein-powder/">organic protein powder guide</a> explains what the organic seal does and does not guarantee.
          </p>
        </div>

